Na$_{2}$SO$_{4}$ has eight known anhydrous phases. For now we have \begin{itemize} \item \href{current_web_home_dir/A2B4C_oC28_63_ac_fg_c-001}{Na$_{2}$SO$_{4}$ III} and \item \href{current_web_home_dir/A2B4C_oF56_70_e_h_a-001}{Thenardite (Na$_{2}$SO$_{4}$ V, $H1_{7}$)} (this structure). \end{itemize} ~\vspace{-.25in} 
  The thenardite phase is ``reported to be stable between 32$^\circ$C and about 180$^\circ$C'' (Nord, 1973), however the data reported here was taken on synthetic thenardite at 25$^\circ$C. 
 The AFLOW label protocol moves the sodium atoms from the reported (16g) site to the (16e) site. This involves shifting the axes so that $\hat{z} \rightarrow \hat{x} \rightarrow \hat{y} \rightarrow \hat{z}$.
